Make the delete-on-exit keep list read the rules table it was replaced by.

Reported against the Windows build: a site added to the keep list is signed out
anyway on exit. The list is not consulted at all, and has not been since the
rules table landed.

aerium-site-rules.patch replaced three site-list prefs with one rules table and
updated the paths that read them - the tab-close cleanup, the session-only
sweep - but not this one. ChromeBrowsingDataLifetimeManager still builds its
shutdown preserve filter from AeriumOnExitPreservedDomains(), which reads
browsing_data::prefs::kAeriumClearOnExitExcludedSites: the legacy pref.

MigrateLegacyLists() copies that pref into kAeriumSiteRules once and sets
kAeriumSiteRulesMigrated. It does not keep the two in step afterwards, and
nothing does, because the Settings table writes only to kAeriumSiteRules. So
every site added through the UI that exists today lands somewhere the exit path
never looks. preserved_domains comes back empty, the code takes its documented
"nothing to keep" branch - a single unfiltered RemoveAndReply - and deletes
everything, including the cookies that keep the user signed in.

It is worse than a list that does nothing, because it is a list that looks like
it works: the entry is in the table, the table persists, and the data is gone
anyway. A profile migrated from before the rules table kept working, which is
why this survived - the legacy pref still held those entries. Anything added
since has been ignored.

So the helper reads the rules table. A rule counts when its list type is kKeep,
which is exactly what the old pref meant; kSession is cleared at shutdown by
definition and kReset at tab close, so neither belongs in a shutdown preserve
filter.

Regex rules are skipped rather than approximated. BrowsingDataFilterBuilder
takes registrable domains, not patterns, and there is no correct conversion -
guessing one could preserve data the user expected to be deleted, which is the
failure that matters in this direction.

Known imprecision, stated rather than left to be found: the exit path applies
one preserve filter across every filterable type it deletes, so a rule that
keeps only some types has all of them preserved on exit. The tab-close path
already groups hosts by keep mask and does this exactly; doing the same here
means splitting the shutdown removal per distinct keep set, which is a larger
change than this fix. The error is toward keeping data on a site the user
explicitly listed, and the common rule - keep everything for this site, which
is what migration writes and what the Add dialog defaults to - is exact.

--- a/chrome/browser/browsing_data/chrome_browsing_data_lifetime_manager.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/browsing_data/chrome_browsing_data_lifetime_manager.cc
@@ -1,20 +1,30 @@
 std::vector<std::string> AeriumOnExitPreservedDomains(PrefService* prefs) {
   std::vector<std::string> result;
-  for (const auto& entry :
-       prefs->GetList(browsing_data::prefs::kAeriumClearOnExitExcludedSites)) {
-    if (!entry.is_string()) {
+  if (!prefs) {
+    return result;
+  }
+  for (const aerium_site_rules::Rule& rule :
+       aerium_site_rules::ReadRules(prefs)) {
+    // Only "never delete". kSession is cleared at shutdown by definition and
+    // kReset at tab close, so neither belongs in a shutdown preserve filter.
+    if (rule.list != aerium_site_rules::ListType::kKeep) {
+      continue;
+    }
+    // A regex rule has no registrable domain to hand a filter builder, and
+    // guessing one could preserve data the user expected to be deleted.
+    if (rule.regex) {
       continue;
     }
-    const std::string& host = entry.GetString();
-    if (host.empty()) {
+    if (rule.host_suffix.empty()) {
       continue;
     }
     std::string domain = net::registry_controlled_domains::GetDomainAndRegistry(
-        host, net::registry_controlled_domains::INCLUDE_PRIVATE_REGISTRIES);
+        rule.host_suffix,
+        net::registry_controlled_domains::INCLUDE_PRIVATE_REGISTRIES);
     if (domain.empty()) {
-      domain = host;  // IP address or internal hostname.
+      domain = rule.host_suffix;  // IP address or internal hostname.
     }
     result.push_back(std::move(domain));
   }
   return result;
 }
